 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. Investors pay a higher standard variable rate than owner-occupiers, and the gap has stayed at about 0.58 percentage points since January 2024.\n\n**Standard variable rates (advertised, banks, latest September 2026):**\n- Investor: **9.35%** p.a.\n- Owner-occupier: **8.77%** p.a.\n- Gap: **0.58 percentage points**\n\nBoth rates have moved together. Each fell from 2025 into early 2026, then rose from February 2026. In January 2024 they were 9.35% (investor) and 8.77% (owner-occupier), the same as today.\n\n**Discounted variable rates (for comparison, September 2026):**\n- Investor: 7.13%\n- Owner-occupier: 6.80%\n- Gap: 0.33 percentage points, down from 0.44 points in January 2024\n\nThese are advertised rates. The RBA notes that rates actually paid on new loans are lower; that data is in table F6, which I can pull if you want the effective rates.",
